.TH STICKY 1
.SH NAME
sticky \- notes in small colored windows, and Markdown shown styled
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sticky
[
.B -n
] [
.B -c
.I color
] [
.B -w
.I n
] [
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sticky
keeps notes, as the Mac's Stickies does, each in a small
.IR rio (1)
window of its own with a pastel background and an acme-like tag
.RI ( adraw (2)).
The notes are
.IR stickyfs (4)'s,
which keeps their text and undo and gives each note one window;
riostart starts it.
With no arguments, sticky opens a window for each note that has none,
starting
.I stickyfs
if none runs, and makes a note if there are none;
with
.B -n
it makes a new one, as
.B New
and Command-N do,
in
.I color
if
.B -c
gives one.
.B -w
.I n
is note
.IR n 's
window, which
.I stickyfs
runs.
A note is the file
.BI $home/lib/sticky/ n .md\fR,
its text in Markdown, so that it reads well in
.IR acme (1),
with its color and its window's rectangle in
.IB n .meta
beside it;
.I stickyfs
serves them there, and keeps them on the disk a moment after each change.
Command-S writes the note at once; there is no
.BR Put .
.B Del
removes the note, its window and its files, at once if the note is empty
and else at a second click, so that one click loses no text.
Removing the file does the same:
the tag's
.B $%
is the note's file, so
.B rm
.B $%
executed in the tag takes the note away.
.B Exit
ends
.I stickyfs
and every window of its, and keeps the notes, so that the next riostart opens them again.
.PP
A note's text edits as an acme window's body does:
button 1 selects, a double click selects a word or the text between brackets or quotes,
the 1-2 and 1-3 chords cut and paste by
.BR /dev/snarf ,
button 2 executes, and button 3 looks, as in
.IR acme (1).
Executed text that is not sticky's own runs in
.IR rc (1),
as the tag runs it, and
.BI | cmd\fR,
.BI < cmd
and
.BI > cmd
act on the note's selection.
A look sends the text to the plumber, or a link's address for a link,
and else finds the text's next occurrence in the note.
Typing replaces the selection;
backspace and control-U and -W erase,
control-A and -E go to the ends of the line,
the arrows move,
Esc selects what was just typed or cuts the selection,
control-X, -C and -V cut, copy and paste,
and control-Z and -R undo and redo the note,
as do
.B Undo
and
.B Redo
and the same keys typed in the tag.
.PP
A double click inside the selection makes it larger:
from a word to its sentence, to its paragraph, list item, quote or code block,
to the section under its heading, and to the whole note.
.B Shrink
gives back the selection it grew from.
.PP
A selection is of the Markdown:
what
.BR Snarf ,
.BR Cut ,
.BI | cmd
and
.BI > cmd
get has its markers, and what
.B Paste
and
.BI < cmd
put in is Markdown, so styles go between notes and to and from acme.
Sticky takes a typed character as itself, and escapes with a backslash one that Markdown
would take as a marker.
.PP
A note's tag starts with only its name and
.BR "Del Snarf |" ;
sticky's words work when typed there and run with button 2:
.BR New ,
.BI Color " name\fR,"
.BR Bold ,
.BR Italic ,
.BR Underline ,
.BR Highlight ,
.BR Strike ,
.BR Shrink ,
.B Source
and
.BR Exit .
The styles turn on for the selection, or off if all of it has them already;
with no selection, they set the style of what you type next.
Keys do the common ones, as the Mac's do:
Command-B, -I and -U bold, italicize and underline,
Command-Shift-X strikes through, as in Notes,
Command-Shift-H highlights,
Command-N makes a note
and Command-S saves.
.B Color
.I name
gives the note a color:
.BR yellow ,
.BR blue ,
.BR green ,
.BR pink ,
.B purple
or
.BR gray ,
or
.BI # rrggbb\fR;
.B Color
alone goes to the next.
The colors are the classic Stickies' pastels, the same in either theme
.RI ( theme (6)),
with dark text and the tag a darker shade of the note's color.
.PP
In Markdown,
.B **bold**
is bold,
.B *italic*
italic,
.B <u>underline</u>
underlined,
.B ==highlight==
highlighted and
.B ~~strike~~
struck through.
Sticky also shows headings, lists, quotes, code spans and fenced blocks,
rules and links, with the markers it shows by style hidden;
what it doesn't show stays as text.
The markers of what holds the selection show, faint: a span's around it,
a link's address, and a heading's, list item's or quote's at the left of its line.
.B Source
shows the Markdown as it is, and again as styled.
.PP
Given a
.IR file ,
or run from the tag of an acme window on a file whose name ends in
.BR .md ,
.I sticky
shows that file.
When there is an acme, it shows the acme window's body, opening a window for the file if
there is none:
each edit in sticky is the smallest change to the window's Markdown, one Undo,
and edits in acme show in sticky as you make them.
.BR Undo ,
.B Redo
and
.B Put
are acme's, and the tag is dirty as the window is.
Sticky ends when acme deletes the window.
While
.I stickyfs
runs, a window on a file is in its index too, and
.B Exit
ends it.
With no acme, sticky edits the file itself, and
.B Put
writes it.
.PP
Sticky draws its text in
.BR $stickyfont ,
one
.IR fontfs (4)
font, by default
.BR /mnt/font/IBMPlex/g/r/12a/font .
Bold, italic and bold italic are its
.BR b ,
.B i
and
.B bi
faces, code is family
.B m
face
.BR r ,
and headings are bold, at 1.4 times its size for level 1, 1.2 times for level 2,
and its size for levels 3 to 6, each to a whole point.
A font that does not open is drawn as plain text is, or as bold is for bold italic;
if
.B $stickyfont
does not open, or is not a
.I fontfs
font, plain text and code are in
.BR $font .
.SH FILES
.TF $home/lib/sticky/*.meta
.TP
.B $home/lib/sticky/*.md
the notes, which
.IR stickyfs (4)
serves there and keeps beneath; riostart opens them at boot, and termrc keeps them when it empties a home
.TP
.B $home/lib/sticky/*.meta
each note's color and rectangle
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/sticky
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (1),
.IR rio (1),
.IR adraw (2),
.IR fontfs (4),
.IR stickyfs (4),
.IR theme (6)
.SH BUGS
A style marker spans one line: sticky marks a selection over lines line by line.
.PP
Cutting and pasting within one note keeps the styles; elsewhere only the text goes.
